.TH FSCRIPT 1 "November 26, 2026" "fast-transcript 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fscript \- transcribe local audio, video, and remote media URLs.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
fscript <media\-or\-url> [destination] [options]
fscript \- [destination] [options]
fscript help [manual]
fscript models
fscript notes TRANSCRIPT
fscript export TRANSCRIPT
fscript agents
fscript skill
fscript permissions
fscript \-\-set\-default\-model NAME
fscript \-\-get\-default\-model
fscript \-\-reset\-default\-model
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Everyday use is \fBfscript video.mp4\fR or \fBfscript
"https://example.com/video"\fR; no
extra flags or subcommands are required. Distinct actions use the same
executable.
Files named after actions use an explicit path such as \fB./notes\fR.
.PP
With a local file or URL, fscript writes a transcript file and prints its
absolute
path to stdout. Progress, warnings, and completion notices go to stderr.
Animated progress
requires an interactive stderr and is disabled with \fBTERM=dumb\fR;
redirected
streams use plain lines. A failed operation stops its progress worker.
The default is speaker\-aware text with timestamps, repetition cleanup,
120\-second
chunks, and two seconds of overlap. The built\-in model is parakeet\-v3\-int8.
A saved model preference or explicit override can select another model.
.PP
Speaker diarization uses FluidAudio when available. If the helper is
unavailable,
the default mode warns and continues with plain transcription. Explicit
\fB\-\-diarize\fR requests require the helper and fail when it is unavailable.
Text modes always disable diarization; conflicting diarization flags produce a
warning.
.PP
Local media decoding requires ffmpeg and ffprobe. Remote URLs use yt\-dlp,
with
uvx yt\-dlp as a fallback. Remote input prefers manual subtitles when
diarization
is disabled; \fB\-\-local\fR forces audio download and local transcription.
The first model download and SDK setup require network access and writable
storage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
fscript lecture.mp3
fscript lecture.mp3 notes/
fscript lecture.mp3 \-\-text=plain \-\-stdout
cat lecture.wav | fscript \- \-\-text=plain
cat lecture.mp3 | fscript \- \-\-no\-diarization \-\-json | jq .text
fscript lecture.mp3 \-\-last 5:00
fscript lecture.mp3 \-\-start 10:00 \-\-end 15:00
fscript 'https://example.com/video' \-\-last 5:00
fscript meeting.m4a \-\-diarize coreml \-\-num\-speakers 2
fscript lecture.mp3 \-\-model ultra \-\-device cpu
fscript lecture.mp3 \-\-text=plain \-\-quiet \-\-output transcript.txt
fscript \-\-list\-models
.fi
.PP
Use \fB\-\fR as input for piped media. Its default destination is stdout. The
command
spools media to a temporary file so decoding and diarization can seek, then
deletes
that file on completion or an ordinary error. Empty stdin or a terminal is
rejected
with a pipeline example. An explicit output file overrides the stdout default;
a directory destination uses \fBstdin\fR as the filename stem.
.PP
Use \fB\-\-\fR before paths that begin with a hyphen. Local filenames are
passed as
absolute paths to ffprobe, ffmpeg, and the diarization helper so those
programs
do not reinterpret them as options. To read a file named help, use
\fBfscript ./help\fR. Bare \fBfscript help\fR displays help.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-speakers[=plain|timestamps]\fR: speaker\-aware text; timestamps are the
default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-text[=plain|compact|timestamps]\fR: text without diarization;
timestamps are the default. Plain writes one segment per line; compact
flattens the transcript.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-json\fR: structured transcript, segments, model metadata, and timings.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-stream\fR: transcribe while media is decoded; live text goes to stdout.
Add \fB\-\-json\fR for versioned JSONL events. Uses two\-second opening chunks
until text is recognized, then eight\-second chunks with half a second of
overlap; an explicit \fB\-\-chunk\fR uses that size throughout. Streaming does
not implicitly enable diarization. Use \fB\-\-diarize\fR for labels after
decoding finishes. An explicit output path saves the final transcript in
addition to the live stdout stream.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-srt\fR, \fB\-\-vtt\fR: experimental subtitle formats.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-o, \-\-output PATH\fR: output file or directory. A trailing slash denotes
a directory even if it does not exist. \fB\-\-output \-\fR writes transcript
contents to stdout.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-stdout\fR: write transcript contents to stdout. A positional
destination of \fB\-\fR is equivalent.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-raw\fR: disable repeated\-word cleanup.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-c, \-\-clean\fR: enable repeated\-word cleanup, the default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-diarize[=coreml|lseend\-dihard3]\fR: require speaker diarization;
defaults to coreml when no value is given. A space before the backend value
also works. The legacy \fB\-d\fR alias remains supported; it does not mean
debug.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-D, \-\-no\-diarization\fR: disable diarization.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-n, \-\-num\-speakers N\fR: expected positive speaker count for coreml.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-t, \-\-threshold N\fR: finite ls\-eend\-dihard3 threshold; defaults to
0.3.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-backend coreml|lseend\-dihard3|none\fR: legacy diarization selection.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-l, \-\-local\fR: download remote audio and transcribe locally.
\fB\-\-prefer\-local\-for\-remote\fR is an alias.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-start TIME\fR: begin at this source timestamp; defaults to 0. TIME
accepts seconds (including decimals), MM:SS, or HH:MM:SS.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-end TIME\fR: stop at this source timestamp, exclusive; defaults to the
end of the media. Must exceed start. Values beyond a known duration are
clamped.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-last TIME\fR: transcribe only the final duration, for example
\fB\-\-last 5:00\fR for the last five minutes. Requires a known finite media
duration and cannot be combined with start/end. A duration longer than the
media selects all of it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-chunk N\fR: chunk duration in seconds; 0 disables chunking.
\fB\-\-chunk\-seconds\fR is an alias.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-overlap N\fR: chunk overlap in seconds; must be smaller than the chunk
duration. \fB\-\-chunk\-overlap\-seconds\fR is an alias.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-model NAME|URL\fR: predefined model or custom http(s) ONNX bundle URL.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-device auto|cpu|mps|mlx|cuda\fR: choose a device supported by the
selected model. The default is auto.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-model\-dir PATH\fR: existing extracted ONNX model or SDK model
directory.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-model\-package PATH\fR: override the cached ONNX tarball path.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-model\-url URL\fR: explicit http(s) ONNX bundle URL.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-list\-models\fR: available models, aliases, benchmark advice, and
device notes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-set\-default\-model NAME\fR: save a predefined model or alias without
downloading it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-get\-default\-model\fR: print the saved model, or the built\-in
default, to stdout.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-reset\-default\-model\fR: clear the saved preference.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-q, \-\-quiet\fR: suppress progress, completion notices, and successful
model\-worker diagnostics. Keep transcript data, result paths, model queries,
warnings, and errors. SDK installers can still report dependency diagnostics
during first setup.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR: full help, common examples, and defaults. Bare invocation
displays a short introduction.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-V, \-\-version\fR: installed version.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-manual\fR: this offline manual. \fBfscript help manual\fR is
equivalent.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-man\-page\fR: export this manual in roff format for man(1).
.PP
Output formats are mutually exclusive. Explicit output paths and stdout are
mutually exclusive. Specify one model selection. Settings commands run on
their
own, with an optional \fB\-\-quiet\fR flag. Their confirmations go to stderr.
Optional output\-mode values also accept a space, for example \fB\-\-text
plain\fR.
When an output path follows an optional\-value flag, use \fB\-\-output PATH\fR
to make
the destination explicit.
.SH "PARTIAL TRANSCRIPTION"
Selection applies to local audio/video, piped media, remote manual subtitles,
and
remote audio downloads. Local decoding seeks and crops before loading the
model;
neither ASR nor diarization sees unselected audio. Piped media is still
spooled
before seeking. Chunk and segment timestamps, including subtitle output,
retain
their original source positions; \fBaudio_seconds\fR measures only selected
audio.
.PP
For remote audio, fscript requests a section using yt\-dlp's
\fB\-\-download\-sections\fR
and accurate cuts rather than downloading the entire media first. This
requires
FFmpeg and a server/protocol that supports seeking. The server can still
transfer
extra data or require earlier fragments; fscript cannot guarantee byte\-range
support from every site. An unknown duration rejects \fB\-\-last\fR before
audio download.
Decoder errors during remote section download fail the command rather than
silently accepting a corrupt or truncated clip.
Ordinary start/end selection works without a known duration.
.PP
Manual captions are filtered by interval overlap and cue times are clipped to
the interval. A boundary cue retains its complete text because captions do not
provide reliable word\-level times. No model or audio download runs on this
path.
If no cues intersect the selection, the caption transcript is empty.
.PP
Selection uses the same default output filename as a whole\-media run. Use a
different destination to retain both versions.
.SH STREAMING
\fBfscript MEDIA \-\-stream\fR prints provisional live text on stdout without
creating
a default output file. Add \fB\-\-json\fR for flushed version 1 JSONL events;
each event
has \fBversion\fR, \fBjob_id\fR, increasing \fBsequence\fR, \fBtype\fR, and
\fBdata\fR fields. Events
are \fBstarted\fR, \fBready\fR, \fBupdate\fR, \fBcompleted\fR, and
\fBerror\fR. Update data contains a
full replacement snapshot (\fBtext\fR, \fBsegments\fR, latest \fBchunk\fR),
not a text delta.
Only \fBcompleted\fR plus exit status 0 proves a successful complete
transcript.
.PP
Final data uses the ordinary cleanup and speaker assignment. Live human output
is raw append\-only text; machine consumers should replace provisional
snapshots
with \fBcompleted.data.result\fR. An explicit destination saves that final
transcript
while keeping live output on stdout. A JSON destination is a JSON document,
not
an event log. Errors leave an existing destination unchanged.
.PP
Local files, ordinary piped media, and resolved HTTP(S) audio streams decode
incrementally. Remote captions still take precedence when eligible;
\fB\-\-local\fR
forces recognition. Last\-duration selection on stdin first spools the input
to
discover its duration. Streaming disables implicit diarization; explicit
\fB\-\-diarize\fR adds labels to the final result after streamed decoding.
.PP
The CLI worker process group is cancelled by Ctrl\-C, SIGTERM, or SIGHUP on
macOS/Linux; helpers stop, scratch files are removed, and exit status is 130.
Cancellation can end JSONL without a terminal event. A closed stdout consumer
stops the job successfully. Use ordinary transcription for SRT/VTT export.
Full protocol and timing definitions:
https://github.com/brenorb/fast\-transcript/blob/main/docs/incremental\-transcription.md
.SH CONFIGURATION
Model selection precedence is explicit \fB\-\-model\fR or
\fB\-\-model\-url\fR, then
\fBFSCRIPT_MODEL_URL\fR, then the saved preference, then the built\-in
default.
The saved model is per user and applies in future shells and scripts.
Use \fB\-\-get\-default\-model\fR to inspect the saved preference; it does not
report an
environment override. An explicit selection bypasses loading the saved
setting.
.PP
Settings live in default\-model.json in the platform configuration directory:
on macOS, ~/Library/Application Support/fast\-transcript; on Linux,
~/.config/fast\-transcript. \fBFSCRIPT_CONFIG_FILE\fR overrides the entire
filename.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFSCRIPT_CONFIG_FILE\fR: default\-model configuration filename.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFSCRIPT_MODEL_DIR\fR: default model directory.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFSCRIPT_MODEL_PACKAGE\fR: default cached ONNX bundle filename.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFSCRIPT_MODEL_URL\fR: model source override, ahead of the saved preference.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFSCRIPT_DIARIZATION_BINARY\fR: explicit FluidAudio executable path or
command name. Without an override, standard installation locations and PATH
are searched.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFSCRIPT_RUNTIME_DIR\fR: persistent location for managed SDK runtimes, when
using an installation that provisions them separately.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFSCRIPT_PYTHON_BINARY\fR: prepared Python interpreter with the selected
model's pinned SDK dependencies.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUV_CACHE_DIR\fR: uv installer cache override.
.SH OUTPUT
Default local filenames are INPUT.speakers.txt, INPUT.transcript.txt,
INPUT.transcript.json, INPUT.srt, or INPUT.vtt, according to the selected
format.
For remote media, filenames use a sanitized title or URL stem in the current
directory. Existing output files are replaced only after the complete
transcript
is staged and synced in the destination directory. A failed write leaves the
earlier transcript intact. Existing file permissions and symlink destinations
are preserved, including
links whose target file must be created. Saving an existing file still
requires
write access to that file. Read\-only files, special files, and symlink loops
are
rejected; choose a writable regular file or stdout instead. New transcript
files are private to the
current user on Unix. Choose another destination to retain earlier results.
.PP
File output prints only the final absolute path to stdout. Stdout mode prints
only the selected transcript representation. JSON is formatted and can be
piped
to jq when stdout is selected. Use \fB\-\-json \-\-stdout\fR for local files,
or \fB\- \-\-json\fR
for piped media. Quiet mode does not remove requested data from stdout. A
consumer that closes
its input early, such as \fBhead\fR, ends stdout delivery successfully without
a
panic or a broken\-pipe diagnostic. Other output failures remain errors.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
Unknown options and nearby model\-name mistakes suggest likely corrections.
Use \fB\-\-help\fR to inspect syntax or \fB\-\-list\-models\fR to choose a
model.
.PP
If saved settings are malformed, run \fBfscript \-\-reset\-default\-model\fR,
or select
\fB\-\-model NAME\fR explicitly for that invocation.
.PP
If audio inspection fails, install ffmpeg and ffprobe and check the input
path.
Missing local files and directory inputs are rejected before model setup or
media
decoding, with a direct path error. This applies to batch and streaming jobs.
For remote download failures, check yt\-dlp or uvx and network access.
.PP
If speaker labels are required, pass \fB\-\-diarize coreml\fR. Set
\fBFSCRIPT_DIARIZATION_BINARY\fR to the installed helper when it cannot be
discovered.
Speaker labels identify voices; they are not people's names.
.PP
If SDK startup fails, install uv or set \fBFSCRIPT_PYTHON_BINARY\fR to a
prepared
interpreter. For offline work, cache dependencies and model weights
beforehand,
then use the same storage paths. \fB\-\-model\-dir PATH\fR selects existing
model files.
Quiet\-mode worker failures retain their last diagnostic messages.
.PP
Press Ctrl\-C to interrupt. Temporary media and normal output are not a resume
checkpoint. An interrupted save leaves the previous transcript intact; abrupt
termination can leave a hidden \fB.fscript\-output\-*\fR staging file beside
it. Remove
that staging file and rerun the command.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Zero indicates success, including help, manual, and version queries. Nonzero
indicates an argument, configuration, dependency, input, transcription, or
output
failure. Scripts should check the exit status before using the result path.
Ordinary failures report their causes concisely on stderr, including when
\fBRUST_BACKTRACE\fR is set; backtraces are reserved for unexpected Rust
panics.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
Documentation: https://github.com/brenorb/fast\-transcript#readme
.PP
Issues: https://github.com/brenorb/fast\-transcript/issues
.SH "ADDITIONAL ACTIONS"
These subcommands are part of the same fscript executable as file/URL
transcription.
.nf
fscript notes TRANSCRIPT [destination] [options]
fscript export TRANSCRIPT [destination] [\-\-format FORMAT] [options]
fscript agents [\-\-json]
fscript agents set NAME
fscript agents add NAME \-\- COMMAND [ARGS...]
fscript agents remove NAME
fscript agents reset
fscript models [\-\-json]
fscript models get [\-\-json]
fscript models set NAME
fscript models reset
fscript skill
fscript skill path
fscript record
fscript record \-\-duration 10
fscript record \-\-control \-\-json
fscript record \-\-hotkey
fscript meeting
fscript meeting \-\-duration 60 \-\-json
fscript meeting watch [\-\-duration SECONDS] [\-\-control] [\-\-json]
fscript meeting auto [status|enable|disable] [\-\-json]
fscript meeting \-\-control
fscript meeting status SESSION [\-\-json]
fscript meeting recover SESSION [\-\-json]
fscript calendar [\-\-json]
fscript calendar status [\-\-json]
fscript calendar connect [\-\-json]
fscript calendar disconnect [\-\-json]
fscript calendar \-\-start RFC3339 \-\-end RFC3339 [\-\-json]
fscript calendar match START END [\-\-json]
fscript permissions [\-\-json]
fscript permissions \-\-request paste [\-\-json]
.fi
.SH "ACTION DEFAULTS"
Notes use an installed Codex, Claude Code, omp, or saved custom command. The
agent
receives transcript data on stdin and returns Markdown. Default selection is
the
saved agent, otherwise the first available built\-in in codex/claude/omp
order.
Agent availability does not verify login. Sign in using the agent's own CLI;
Fast Transcript never starts an interactive login flow.
.SH "ACTION OPTIONS"
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-agent NAME\fR: override the selected note agent; auto uses automatic
detection.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-model NAME\fR: override a built\-in agent's language model. Custom
commands own their model arguments.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-input\-format auto|text|json|jsonl\fR: transcript format; defaults to
automatic detection.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-o, \-\-output PATH\fR: notes file or directory, instead of the optional
destination operand.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-stdout\fR: print notes without saving a file. A destination of \- is
equivalent.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-timeout SECONDS\fR: positive agent execution timeout; defaults to 300
seconds.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-json\fR: print completion metadata including the Markdown notes and
optional saved path.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-q, \-\-quiet\fR: suppress status; keep results and errors.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR: command help and examples. Nested commands support help.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-V, \-\-version\fR: installed control CLI version.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-manual\fR: complete offline control manual.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-man\-page\fR: export this manual in roff format.
.SH "ACTION OUTPUT"
File input saves INPUT.notes.md and prints its absolute path. Piped input
defaults
to stdout. A JSON transcript or complete JSONL stream retains source
timestamps
and speakers; a provisional or failed stream is rejected. With \-\-json,
stdout is
one completion object instead of a path; the default Markdown file is still
saved
unless \-\-stdout or piped\-input defaults select stdout. Failed generation
leaves
an existing destination unchanged and cannot overwrite the source transcript.
.SH "AGENT SKILL"
Run fscript skill to print the bundled SKILL.md as Markdown. It needs no
model,
login, network, or configuration and does not install anything. For tools that
need a file path, fscript skill path materializes the same bundled content in
the application configuration directory under
skills/fast\-transcript/SKILL.md.
FSCRIPT_SKILL_DIR overrides that cache directory. With \-\-json, skill returns
version, name, content, and an optional path. This follows Say It's skill/path
convention while keeping bare skill useful with no additional arguments.
.SH "PASTE PERMISSIONS"
\fBfscript permissions\fR inspects automatic\-paste access for the running
process.
It never prompts, opens Settings, posts keys, or starts capture/inference.
With \-\-json, it returns version 1 and paste.supported/paste.granted
booleans;
inspection succeeds even when access is absent or the platform is unsupported.
Only the explicit \-\-request paste action asks macOS for event\-posting
access and
may show its permission prompt. The response reports the effective grant after
the request; requesting access does not imply success. Unsupported requests
fail.
No permission is reset, disabled, or automatically granted. Grants depend on
the
responsible app/terminal and code identity, so inspect/request from the same
execution path that will deliver text. An enabled Settings entry for an older
ad\-hoc build may not authorize the current build. Export \-\-paste stays
noninteractive and fails clearly if access is absent; \-\-copy remains
available.
.SH "MICROPHONE DICTATION"
Run fscript record to capture the default macOS microphone and print the
finished
plain transcript. Ctrl\-C finishes capture and transcribes; a second Ctrl\-C
cancels.
SIGTERM/SIGHUP cancel immediately with status 130. For unattended use, supply
\-\-duration SECONDS, or \-\-control for explicit JSONL
start/stop/cancel/shutdown
requests on stdin. Control mode keeps the loaded model across recordings.
\-\-json selects version 1 lifecycle events; control mode always emits those
events.
\-\-output\-dir DIR saves completed JSON documents in unique child
directories.
Microphone audio is temporary and is removed on completion or cancellation.
Model/device overrides reuse the file\-transcription configuration. Help never
opens the microphone or loads a model. See docs/recording.md for the protocol,
permissions, platform coverage, and validation.
.PP
With \-\-hotkey, record stays warm and captures while the selected global
shortcut
is held. Auto chooses the first available exclusive registration:
Control\-Shift\-
Space, Control\-Shift\-D, then Control\-Shift\-Period. The ready event reports
shortcut_label and shortcut_available. Supply \-\-hotkey ctrl+shift+d (or the
other
listed chords) to choose explicitly. Shortcut mode may be combined with
control;
a failed registration then leaves control recording usable and reports
shortcut_error. Without control, registration failure exits nonzero. Ctrl\-C
cancels shortcut mode; it does not finalize an ongoing clip.
.SH "AGENT SETTINGS"
Use fscript agents set NAME to save a built\-in or custom agent, or agents set
auto to clear
the preference. Agents add stores a literal program/argument array; no shell
is
used. Custom commands read the prompt on stdin and write only Markdown to
stdout.
They run in an isolated temporary working directory, so use absolute argument
paths. Agents remove deletes a custom profile and clears its default if
selected.
Agents reset removes only the note\-agent settings, leaving ASR models and
agent
logins untouched. Put global \-\-json before the custom command's \-\-
separator.
.PP
Settings live in agents.json in the same platform configuration directory as
the
ASR preference. FSCRIPT_NOTES_CONFIG_FILE overrides that filename.
FSCRIPT_AGENT_PATH
replaces executable search paths; otherwise PATH and standard
user/Homebrew/nvm
locations are searched. FSCRIPT_CODEX_BINARY, FSCRIPT_CLAUDE_BINARY, and
FSCRIPT_OMP_BINARY override built\-in executables.
.SH MODELS
Models uses the same catalog as fscript \-\-list\-models. Models set accepts
the same names and aliases as fscript \-\-set\-default\-model and saves to the
same
configuration file. Models reset clears only the saved preference and can
recover
malformed settings. These commands never download a model or start inference.
.PP
Models get prints the effective model: FSCRIPT_MODEL_URL, otherwise the saved
preference, otherwise parakeet\-v3\-int8. This differs from the compatibility
flag
fscript \-\-get\-default\-model, which reports the saved or built\-in choice
only.
With \-\-json, each models command returns version 1, saved_default (or null),
environment_override (or null), effective_model, and the shared models array.
Each catalog entry includes name, repository, url, revision, runtime, and
advice.
Explicit per\-transcription \-\-model still takes precedence over these
defaults.
.SH "TRANSCRIPT EXPORT"
Export reads a full fscript JSON transcript, a completed event, or a complete
version 1 JSONL stream from a file or stdin. It uses the same Rust renderer as
transcription and never loads a model or decodes media. Plain text input
cannot
recover segment timing and is rejected by transcript formats. Incomplete or
failed streams are rejected.
.PP
The default format is text (timestamped); \-\-format also accepts plain,
compact,
speakers (timestamped), srt, vtt, and json. Repetition cleanup follows the
usual
transcription default. File input saves a sibling with the selected extension
and
prints its absolute path. Stdin defaults to stdout; \-\-stdout or destination
\- also
prints the content. A destination file/directory or \-o overrides the default.
Existing destinations are replaced atomically after successful rendering;
source
transcripts cannot be overwritten. With \-\-json, stdout is version 1
completion
metadata with content and output_path. To obtain a transcript JSON document,
use \-\-format json \-\-stdout; \-\-json itself selects the metadata envelope.
Content is the renderer's string; file/stdout delivery adds a trailing
newline.
.PP
Export validation compares all seven formats with batch output or saved JSON,
compares document/completed\-event/JSONL input, and checks failed writes
preserve
existing files. A 500\-case Unicode property test checks that serializing and
reloading transcripts preserves all seven renderings.
JSON float parsing preserves round\-trip timestamp precision.
.PP
Use \-\-format markdown to export existing UTF\-8 notes without calling an
agent or
parsing transcript JSON. It preserves the notes, including existing final
blank
lines, and adds a final newline when absent. Its default destination is
INPUT.export.md. The same atomic writer, source\-overwrite guard, stdout, and
JSON
metadata options apply. Empty Markdown is rejected.
.SH "CLIPBOARD DELIVERY"
Export \-\-copy copies the chosen rendering instead of saving another export
file.
The rendered content remains on stdout (or in the \-\-json completion
envelope),
and output_path is null. \-\-paste additionally sends Command\-V to the
positive
\-\-target\-pid process ID; specifying that explicit target is required. Both
flags
conflict with output paths. Paste preflights macOS event\-posting access for
the process performing delivery
(CGPreflightPostEventAccess) and waits
up to one second for held modifiers to be released. It reports key delivery,
not an acknowledgement that the receiving application accepted the paste.
.PP
Clipboard delivery uses /usr/bin/pbcopy on macOS. FSCRIPT_CLIPBOARD_BINARY may
point to a literal stdin\-to\-clipboard executable (also on other platforms),
without shell evaluation. Failure returns nonzero and never modifies the
source
transcript. The existing transcript remains available if copying/pasting
fails.
Clipboard export jobs use the shared cancellation supervisor. The desktop
checks
that the original app is still active before requesting automatic paste; CLI
callers supplying target\-pid own that focus decision. Default automatic paste
is
off. These APIs are also usable noninteractively by agents.
.SH "ERRORS AND CANCELLATION"
Success is 0; errors are nonzero. Diagnostics use stderr. Notes jobs share the
streaming CLI's process\-group supervisor on macOS/Linux. Ctrl\-C, SIGTERM, or
SIGHUP
returns 130, stops owned helpers, and removes job scratch files. Timeouts fail
without replacing existing notes. The caller must provide a complete
transcript;
literal JSON\-like prose can be forced with \-\-input\-format text.
.SH "CALL INSPECTION"
\fBfscript calls\fR inspects one snapshot of native call\-app audio activity
without
recording, models, calendar access or permission prompts. \-\-json supplies
raw
active audio\-process metadata and same\-app microphone/output candidates.
\-\-watch confirms five seconds of same\-app input/output activity, emits one
start
per call, and ends after twenty seconds of observed microphone absence.
Unknown
observations and long observation gaps reset timing. \-\-duration SECONDS
requires
\-\-watch and stops inspection without a terminal. \-\-json with \-\-watch
emits flushed,
versioned JSONL snapshots, started/ended events and a terminal completed
event.
Ctrl\-C cancels inspection. This command does not automatically record calls.
Native inspection requires macOS 14.2+. See docs/call\-detection.md for
coverage.
.SH "LIVE MEETING SESSIONS"
\fBfscript meeting\fR records the default microphone and system output into
separate,
private tracks under a new child of ./meetings and shows channel\-attributed
live
preview on stderr. On success, stdout prints only the absolute completed
transcript
path. \-\-quiet hides preview/status without removing this path. It uses the
shared
saved model. The capture service requires macOS 14.2+
and OS microphone/system\-audio access. The desktop uses these same commands.
Native capture passed local acceptance on built\-in devices; see
docs/live\-meetings.md for the protocol and hardware coverage limits.
.PP
\-\-duration SECONDS stops capture without a terminal. \-\-output\-dir DIR
chooses the
parent session directory; each recording creates a new child and never
overwrites
an existing session. \-\-model, \-\-model\-dir, and \-\-device use the media
CLI's shared
selection. \-\-json emits flushed version 1 session events. Ctrl\-C finishes
ordinary
capture and drains pending recognition; a second interrupt cancels. In
\-\-control
mode, Ctrl\-C cancels immediately.
.PP
\-\-control waits for one newline\-terminated JSON start request on stdin.
Send stop
to stop capture and finish pending transcription, or cancel/shutdown to exit
without a final transcript. Closing stdin before stop interrupts the session;
closing it after stop permits normal completion. Requests use the same action
names as dictation, but one meeting process handles exactly one session. A
start
request during an active session is an error. Each line is limited to 64 KiB.
.PP
Audio is written/checkpointed independently of model loading and inference.
Recognition failure reports recognition_error and keeps capture running until
stop/duration; saved confirms that audio is durable, not that recognition
finished.
Live snapshots use transcript.partial.json with short context windows. Final
recognition uses 30\-second windows with 2\-second overlap on the same loaded
model,
including windows processed during capture. It may revise earlier preview
words.
Only successful final recognition publishes transcript.json and a completed
event,
using the existing document
schema so export and notes need no meeting\-specific rendering logic.
Microphone
and System are source channels, not inferred identities of meeting
participants.
.PP
meeting status SESSION inspects the last durable manifest without loading a
model
or opening devices. meeting recover SESSION retains every complete saved
sample
from an interrupted, unowned recording, marks it interrupted, and refuses a
live
owner. Recovery does not resume capture or claim a complete transcript.
.PP
meeting transcribe SESSION repairs an ownerless recording when necessary and
transcribes its retained audio without opening devices. Use \-\-model,
\-\-device,
or \-\-model\-dir after transcribe to override the same speech\-model
preference.
No terminal is needed; Ctrl\-C cancels immediately. \-\-json emits session
events,
including restored with the saved manifest, then model_ready and completed.
There is no live preview pass. The shared final recognition profile is used;
preview_transcribe_seconds is zero. Default stdout is the absolute final path;
\-\-quiet suppresses status. Capture options cannot be combined with a
subcommand.
.PP
The session lock covers recording and final publication, or the entire saved
transcription. An owned session refuses another recovery/transcription
attempt.
Saved tracks must match their stopped/interrupted manifest. A successful retry
atomically replaces transcript.json; cancellation/failure preserves any
previous
transcript and raw audio. Completed meeting documents include optional meeting
metadata containing the capture manifest, whose state and track_frames
distinguish
successful capture from retained interrupted audio. A completed transcript of
an
interrupted recording does not establish complete capture of the meeting.
.PP
Completed meeting documents include optional preview_transcribe_seconds, which
is
already included in transcribe_seconds but is not represented by the final
chunk
list. Final chunk times plus preview time equal total inference time on both
tracks. Backend\-reported model load is counted once. Engine calls include
their
runtime
audio\-file/IPC work; capture waiting, capture\-side normalization/storage,
and
external notes are excluded. The completed document replaces preview text
rather than extending its prefix.
.PP
Meeting capture follows default input/output UID and hardware\-format changes.
Known transient failures use bounded reconnect attempts; idle system output
alone
is not a stall. source_status events describe ready/reconnecting/receiving
sources.
The optional result.meeting.route_interruptions array preserves uncertain
coverage
and its resolution. Reconnection resolves only when replacement PCM is
retained.
A stopped recording can contain stopped_during_reconnect or
no_post_route_audio
intervals; consult the manifest instead of assuming every stopped recording
has
complete coverage. Permanent/exhausted capture failures prevent completion and
checkpoint interrupted state when storage remains healthy.
Crashes/cancellation
can leave recording for explicit recovery. See docs/live\-meetings.md for the
retry,
clock, publication, and native\-acceptance limits.
.SH "CALENDAR CONTEXT"
Bare fscript calendar lists the next 24 hours from all locally visible
EventKit
calendars. Supply both \-\-start and \-\-end RFC 3339 timestamps with
timezones for
another complete period. Status reads only authorization and naming
preference.
Inspection never prompts or loads a model. Without full access, listing
returns
queried:false; match fails instead of implying no matching event.
.PP
Only calendar connect requests macOS full calendar access (OS read/write
scope;
Fast Transcript only reads metadata) and enables meeting names. Disconnect
disables
future naming without revoking OS access or removing saved titles. Background
naming never requests access. Unsupported inspection succeeds; Connect
requires
macOS 14.2 or later. Ctrl\-C cancels a pending request.
.PP
Calendar match START END selects the largest full\-recording overlap among
titled,
non\-all\-day, non\-cancelled, non\-declined events with another attendee or
call link.
It requires at least one\-quarter overlap of the shorter interval, then breaks
ties
by shorter event, earlier start and stable IDs. Calendar times never start
capture.
The optional manifest.calendar_event is persisted before final recognition and
included in saved/restored events and result.meeting. Existing names survive
replay
and disconnect. Read failures warn without losing audio or failing
recognition.
.PP
With \-\-json, calendar returns one version\-1 object: supported,
authorization,
name_meetings, queried, window, calendars, events, matched_event. Dates are
UTC
milliseconds. Listing retains personal/all\-day/declined events and
inventories the
complete requested period; only match filters eligibility. Only match fills
matched_event. No participant identities, links or event notes are stored.
FSCRIPT_CALENDAR_CONFIG_FILE overrides the private calendar.json preferences.
See docs/calendar.md for fields, matching rules, native permission and
coverage.
.SH "AUTOMATIC MEETINGS"
fscript meeting watch explicitly arms foreground automatic recording. It uses
the
shared process\-attributed detector, starts the existing meeting service once
per
continuous confirmed call episode, and stops only automatically owned capture
after twenty seconds of confirmed absence. Overlapping apps share one
recording.
Calendar times never start capture. Default stdout prints completed transcript
paths; preview/status uses stderr. \-\-quiet also reaches the meeting child.
.PP
\-\-duration limits watching then finishes active capture and final
recognition.
Ctrl\-C cancels the entire supervised group and retains saved audio. One
watcher
per settings profile and one live meeting through final recognition prevent
parallel automatic/manual capture. A manual recording already covering a call
is
left running and consumes that episode. Stop cannot restart the same call.
Failure/cancellation disarms that watch generation; restart explicitly.
.PP
\-\-control emits offers without opening capture/model. Send
newline\-terminated
JSON accept/skip/stop/cancel requests with the offered episode, or
suppress/shutdown
without an episode. Only a current unconsumed accept can start a session. A
GUI
must reserve its workspace before accepting. Each outer meeting event contains
episode and the unchanged inner meeting event; decode each episode
independently.
All outer events have version 1 and contiguous sequence. Watch EOF/completion
is
not evidence of an inner transcript's success. See docs/automatic\-meetings.md
for
the complete protocol, ownership, fixture boundaries and remaining native
checks.
.PP
meeting auto defaults to status. Enable/disable save the desktop startup
preference
without capture, model loading or permission prompts; they do not start a
daemon.
Watch is an explicit opt\-in independent of that preference. Override the
shared
meetings.json preference/lock profile with FSCRIPT_MEETING_CONFIG_FILE. The
desktop gear/menu toggle uses this preference and one controlled watcher. It
reserves the workspace before accepting an offer; disabling finishes active
capture.
Cancel disables the saved preference, while Quit preserves it for future
startup.
